# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# .gitattributes — MDT-OEM-Extensibility
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# This file tells Git how to handle line endings, encoding hints, and merge
# strategy per file type. It exists because this repository mixes several
# content types — Windows text files, POSIX text files, binary assets, and
# configuration files with specific encoding requirements — and a single
# global rule would be wrong for at least one of them.
#
# The rules below are grouped by concern:
#
#   1. Default behavior
#   2. Windows text files (PowerShell, CMD, XML, and so on)
#   3. Registry files (UTF-16 LE, CRLF)
#   4. Cross-platform text files (Markdown, JSON, YAML, and so on)
#   5. Binary files (images, archives, installers)
#   6. Language and format hints
#   7. Export-ignore rules for release archives
#
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════


# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 1. DEFAULT BEHAVIOR
# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# When a file's type is not explicitly handled below, let Git normalize
# line endings to LF in the repository and check them out with the platform
# default (CRLF on Windows, LF on Linux and macOS). This is the modern Git
# default and the safest baseline.

* text=auto


# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 2. WINDOWS TEXT FILES
# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Files that are consumed by Windows tools which expect CRLF line endings
# and UTF-8 (or UTF-16) encoding without BOM.

# PowerShell — CRLF for Windows PowerShell 5.1 compatibility
*.ps1       text eol=crlf
*.psm1      text eol=crlf
*.psd1      text eol=crlf
*.ps1xml    text eol=crlf

# Batch and command scripts — CRLF
*.cmd       text eol=crlf
*.bat       text eol=crlf

# Windows Unattend and DISM XML — CRLF
*.xml       text eol=crlf

# Windows INI files — CRLF
*.ini       text eol=crlf

# Winget manifests and Microsoft-authored YAML — CRLF
*.winget    text eol=crlf


# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 3. REGISTRY FILES
# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# .reg files must be UTF-16 LE with a BOM and CRLF line endings. Git cannot
# enforce encoding, but it can enforce line endings. The encoding rule is
# documented in CONTRIBUTING.md and must be applied by the contributor.

*.reg       text eol=crlf working-tree-encoding=UTF-16LE-BOM


# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 4. CROSS-PLATFORM TEXT FILES
# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Files that are read by tools and services on any platform. These should
# use LF in the repository and on disk, regardless of the checkout platform,
# so that diffs and hashes are stable across environments.

# Markdown
*.md        text eol=lf

# JSON — recipes, schemas, map files
*.json      text eol=lf

# YAML — GitHub configuration, workflows
*.yml       text eol=lf
*.yaml      text eol=lf

# Plain text — notes, configs, license files
*.txt       text eol=lf

# Git configuration files
.gitattributes  text eol=lf
.gitignore      text eol=lf
.gitconfig      text eol=lf

# Environment files and shell scripts
*.sh        text eol=lf
*.env       text eol=lf


# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 5. BINARY FILES
# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Files that must never be normalized, diffed as text, or merged as text.
# Marking them binary tells Git to leave the bytes alone.

# Images
*.jpg       binary
*.jpeg      binary
*.png       binary
*.gif       binary
*.bmp       binary
*.ico       binary
*.tif       binary
*.tiff      binary
*.webp      binary
*.svg       text eol=lf

# Archives
*.zip       binary
*.7z        binary
*.7z.001    binary
*.7z.002    binary
*.7z.003    binary
*.7z.004    binary
*.7z.005    binary
*.7z.006    binary
*.7z.007    binary
*.7z.008    binary
*.7z.009    binary
*.7z.010    binary
*.rar       binary
*.tar       binary
*.gz        binary
*.tgz       binary
*.bz2       binary
*.xz        binary

# Executables and installers
*.exe       binary
*.msi       binary
*.msp       binary
*.msu       binary
*.msix      binary
*.msixbundle binary
*.appx      binary
*.appxbundle binary
*.dll       binary
*.sys       binary
*.com       binary
*.scr       binary

# Driver and firmware files
*.inf       text eol=crlf
*.cat       binary
*.bin       binary
*.rom       binary
*.efi       binary
*.cap       binary
*.fd        binary

# Disk and OS images
*.iso       binary
*.wim       binary
*.esd       binary
*.swm       binary
*.vhd       binary
*.vhdx      binary
*.vmdk      binary
*.vdi       binary
*.ffu       binary

# Fonts and other binaries
*.ttf       binary
*.otf       binary
*.woff      binary
*.woff2     binary

# Certificate and key files (should never be committed, but if they slip in)
*.pfx       binary
*.p12       binary
*.key       binary
*.pem       text eol=lf


# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 6. LANGUAGE AND FORMAT HINTS
# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Tell GitHub Linguist how to classify files for repository statistics.
# This does not affect Git behavior — only how the language bar renders.

# Vendored or generated content that should not count toward language stats
vendors/**/Assets/**/*.js    linguist-vendored
vendors/**/Assets/**/*.css   linguist-vendored

# Documentation and configuration do not count as code
docs/**      linguist-documentation
.github/**   linguist-documentation

# Recipes are data, not code
vendors/**/Recipe.json  linguist-documentation

# Schemas are configuration
schema/**   linguist-documentation


# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 7. EXPORT-IGNORE RULES
# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Files that should be excluded from `git archive` and GitHub's automatic
# release tarballs. These are developer-only files that users do not need.

.github/          export-ignore
.gitattributes    export-ignore
.gitignore        export-ignore
.editorconfig     export-ignore
tests/            export-ignore
CONTRIBUTING.md   export-ignore
CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md export-ignore
CHANGELOG.md      export-ignore


# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# END OF .gitattributes
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# If you add a new file type to the repository, consider whether it needs
# an explicit rule here. The default `* text=auto` covers most cases, but
# Windows-specific and binary files benefit from being called out.
#
# For more on gitattributes syntax, see:
#   https://git-scm.com/docs/gitattributes
